```

States: idle → loading → success / error → idle again

50%+ of web traffic is mobile. Design for the small screen first:

```css
/* Mobile first: base styles are for small screens */
.input-row { flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
.submit-btn { width: 100%; }

/* Then expand for larger screens */
@media (min-width: 640px) {
  .input-row { flex-direction: row; }
  .submit-btn { width: auto; }
}
```

### Quick wins for mobile:
- Minimum tap target: 44×44px (Apple's guideline)
- Font size ≥ 16px on inputs (prevents iOS zoom)
- Avoid horizontal scroll
- Test by resizing browser window to 375px wide

### Three states, every async action

Every component that calls an API has (at minimum) three states: idle, loading, and either success or error. Missing any of them creates a confusing UI.

```jsx
function Summarizer() {
  const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle'); // idle | loading | success | error
  const [result, setResult] = useState('');
  const [error, setError] = useState('');

  async function handleSubmit(text) {
    setStatus('loading');
    try {
      const res = await fetch('/api/summarize', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ text })
      });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Server returned ${res.status}`);
      const data = await res.json();
      setResult(data.summary);
      setStatus('success');
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message);
      setStatus('error');
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => handleSubmit(text)} disabled={status === 'loading'}>
        {status === 'loading' ? 'Summarizing…' : 'Summarize'}
      </button>
      {status === 'error' && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
      {status === 'success' && <p>{result}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Bad vs. good error messages

**Bad:** 'Error.' / 'Something went wrong.' / raw stack trace shown to the user.

**Good:** tells the user what happened, in plain language, and what to do about it.

```jsx
function getErrorMessage(err) {
  if (err.message.includes('429')) {
    return 'Too many requests right now. Wait a moment and try again.';
  }
  if (err.message.includes('401')) {
    return 'Your session expired. Please refresh the page.';
  }
  if (err.message.includes('Failed to fetch')) {
    return 'Can\'t reach the server. Check your connection and try again.';
  }
  return 'Something went wrong on our end. Try again in a moment.';
}
```

Map technical errors to specific, actionable sentences. Only fall back to a generic message when you genuinely don't know what happened — and even then, tell the user to retry, not just that something broke.

