contentSections.body:
The signal worth trusting isn't "how long is this file" — it's "does this chunk of JSX have an identity independent of the thing around it, and would I want it somewhere else without dragging the rest along." A form's submit handler and its fields are one coherent story about submitting; a list item rendering one `Brief` is a different, self-contained story that happens to currently live next to the first one.

```tsx
// components/BriefCard.tsx
type BriefCardProps = { brief: Brief };

export function BriefCard({ brief }: BriefCardProps) {
  return (
    <li>
      <h3>{brief.title}</h3>
      <p>{brief.summary}</p>
      <span>{brief.deliverables.length} deliverables</span>
    </li>
  );
}
```

A good extraction hides internals behind a small, typed props interface — `BriefCard` doesn't know or care that its data came from a fetch call, and `page.tsx` doesn't need to know how a brief is rendered, only that it has one to hand off. That's what makes the extraction actually reduce complexity instead of just relocating the same 12 lines to a different file with an import statement now required to read them.

The fix is lifting the shared state to the nearest common ancestor of the components that need it — here, the page or layout that renders both `<BriefList />` and `<WeeklyCount />` — and passing it down as props instead of letting each component fetch and store its own copy:

```tsx
// app/page.tsx — the nearest common ancestor of BriefList and WeeklyCount
export default function Page() {
  const [briefs, setBriefs] = useState<Brief[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/briefs').then(r => r.json()).then(setBriefs);
  }, []);

  function addBrief(brief: Brief) {
    setBriefs(prev => [brief, ...prev]);
  }

  return (
    <>
      <WeeklyCount briefs={briefs} />
      <BriefList briefs={briefs} onNewBrief={addBrief} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Now there's exactly one array of briefs, living in exactly one place. `WeeklyCount` and `BriefList` both read from it, and a submit updates it once, in the one place it lives — both components see the new count on the same render, because they're not looking at two different numbers anymore, they're looking at the same one.

The size of the jump matters more than the number of consumers. Two components three lines apart in the same file sharing a value is a props problem — passing `briefs={briefs}` down is not meaningfully more work than a Context provider, and it's easier to trace: anyone reading `<WeeklyCount briefs={briefs} />` can see exactly where that data comes from without knowing Context exists. A value needed in a header, a footer, and a settings page — three places with no shared nearby parent except the entire app — is where prop drilling actually starts hurting, and where Context earns the machinery it costs:

```tsx
// lib/user-context.tsx — only reached for once the nearby-parent option doesn't exist
const UserContext = createContext<User | null>(null);

export function UserProvider({ user, children }: { user: User; children: ReactNode }) {
  return <UserContext.Provider value={user}>{children}</UserContext.Provider>;
}

export function useUser() {
  const user = useContext(UserContext);
  if (!user) throw new Error('useUser must be used inside UserProvider');
  return user;
}
```

The rule that generalizes: reach for lifted state and props first, always — it's simpler to read and requires zero new abstractions. Reach for Context specifically when the components that need a value have no useful common ancestor to lift it to, not as a default starting point for anything shared.

The App Router turns that decision directly into file structure — a folder under `app/` with a `page.tsx` in it becomes a route, and `[id]` in a folder name becomes a dynamic segment:

```
app/
  page.tsx                # /            — the intake form + brief list
  briefs/
    [id]/
      page.tsx             # /briefs/123 — one brief's detail view
```

```tsx
// app/briefs/[id]/page.tsx
export default async function BriefDetailPage({ params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }) {
  const { id } = await params;
  const brief = await getBrief(id);
  if (!brief) notFound();

  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{brief.title}</h1>
      <DeliverablesList deliverables={brief.deliverables} />
    </article>
  );
}
```

The sort toggle inside that page stays exactly what it was — a `useState` on a client component nested in the detail page, changing what's shown without ever touching the URL, because nobody needs `/briefs/123?sort=priority` to exist as a shareable address.

