The rule of three, applied here: write the first brief type as straightforward code. When a second brief type shows up — say, a logo-design brief needing different fields than a general project brief — write it as its own straightforward code too, even if it duplicates some structure from the first. Only on the third similar case do you have enough real examples to see which parts of the pattern are genuinely shared and which only looked shared with two data points:

```tsx
// Before a third case exists — two brief types, two components, some duplication, and that's fine
function ProjectBriefView({ brief }: { brief: ProjectBrief }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{brief.title}</h1>
      <Section title="Deliverables">{brief.deliverables.map(d => <li key={d}>{d}</li>)}</Section>
      <Section title="Timeline">{brief.timeline}</Section>
    </article>
  );
}

function LogoBriefView({ brief }: { brief: LogoBrief }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{brief.title}</h1>
      <Section title="Style references">{brief.styleRefs.map(r => <li key={r}>{r}</li>)}</Section>
      <Section title="Formats needed">{brief.formats.join(', ')}</Section>
    </article>
  );
}
```

Duplication between two real, known cases is cheap and honest — it's visible, it's easy to change independently if they diverge further, and it costs nothing but a few repeated lines. A guessed abstraction built for cases that don't exist yet costs a wrong schema, extra indirection, and a harder second feature — for a savings that hasn't been earned.

