The fix isn't a more careful client-side key — it's removing the API key from the browser's reach entirely. Anthropic's SDK, called from a Next.js Route Handler (server-side code that runs only on the server, never shipped to the browser), can read a plain, non-`NEXT_PUBLIC_` environment variable that the client never sees:

```ts
// app/api/brief/route.ts — runs only on the server
import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';

const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY });

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { clientNotes } = await req.json();
  const message = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-sonnet-4-5',
    max_tokens: 1024,
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: `Turn these client notes into a structured brief:\n${clientNotes}` }],
  });
  return Response.json({ brief: message.content[0] });
}
```

`process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix) is available only inside code that runs on the server — Route Handlers, Server Components, Server Actions. Next.js never bundles it into anything the browser downloads. The browser now only ever talks to `/api/brief`, briefdesk's own endpoint — it never learns the Anthropic key exists at all.

