The Anthropic SDK accepts a `timeout` option per request, in milliseconds, after which it throws rather than waiting indefinitely:

```ts
const message = await client.messages.create(
  {
    model: 'claude-sonnet-4-5',
    max_tokens: 1024,
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: prompt }],
  },
  { timeout: 30_000 },   // 30 seconds — briefdesk's own deadline, not Anthropic's default
);
```

Thirty seconds is a real design decision, not an arbitrary number: it's long enough for a genuinely complex brief to finish generating, and short enough that a user isn't staring at a spinner for minutes wondering if the tab needs to be closed. The route's `catch` block, from the earlier lesson on shaping errors, already handles this the same way it handles a rate-limit or a 500 — the timeout just becomes one more reason that catch block fires, with its own plain-language message: "That took too long — try again, or shorten your notes."

The pattern from the CLI-tools phase's caching-and-fallback module applies directly here: retry only failures where the same request has a real chance of succeeding on a second try — a rate limit or a temporary overload is exactly that kind of failure, since the condition causing it (too much traffic right now) can genuinely be gone a second later. A malformed request or an invalid key will fail identically every time, because nothing about the request changed between attempts.

```ts
async function callClaudeWithRetry(params: MessageCreateParams, maxRetries = 2) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await client.messages.create(params, { timeout: 30_000 });
    } catch (err) {
      const retryable = err instanceof Anthropic.APIError && (err.status === 429 || err.status === 529 || err.status >= 500);
      if (!retryable || attempt === maxRetries) throw err;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 500 * 2 ** attempt));  // backoff: 500ms, then 1000ms
    }
  }
}
```

Checking `err.status` before retrying is what prevents the malformed-request case from tripling its own failure — a `400` throws immediately on the first attempt, while a `429` gets a second chance after a short, increasing delay (exponential backoff), giving whatever caused the rate limit a real window to clear.

A simple per-user (or per-IP, for anonymous users) rate limit on the route itself catches this long before it becomes thousands of calls:

```ts
// lib/rate-limit.ts — a minimal in-memory limiter (fine for a single-server deploy;
// a multi-instance deploy needs a shared store like Redis instead)
const requestLog = new Map<string, number[]>();

export function isRateLimited(key: string, maxRequests = 5, windowMs = 60_000): boolean {
  const now = Date.now();
  const timestamps = (requestLog.get(key) ?? []).filter(t => now - t < windowMs);
  if (timestamps.length >= maxRequests) return true;
  timestamps.push(now);
  requestLog.set(key, timestamps);
  return false;
}
```

```ts
// app/api/brief/route.ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const userId = await getUserId(req);  // from session/auth
  if (isRateLimited(userId, 5, 60_000)) {
    return Response.json({ error: 'Too many requests. Wait a minute and try again.' }, { status: 429 });
  }
  // ... existing validation and Claude call
}
```

Five requests per minute is a real number chosen for briefdesk's actual usage — a person writing and regenerating one brief at a time never needs more than that; a runaway `useEffect` firing on every keystroke hits the ceiling in well under a second and gets a `429` instead of a few thousand more billed calls.

